{"id":"closing-not-opening","title":"Closing balances that are not the next year's opening balances","question":"When one return closes the year with a figure, does the next return open with the same figure, and does it repeat last year's revenue and spending as they were filed?","finding":"Among 1,851 pairs of consecutive returns, 47 pairs from 39 organizations disagree by $25,000 or more (and 1% of spending) on net assets, total assets, or last year's revenue or expenses. The largest is Brilliant Corners, for the year ended 30 June 2022: a change of $263.5 million between what was filed and what the next return says was filed. 31 pairs carry no amended return, prior-period adjustment or other sign of why.","how":"For each pair of returns whose tax years follow one another without a break: net assets and total assets at the end of the first, against the opening figures on the second; and total revenue and expenses on the first, against the prior-year column on the second. Where a year has an amended return, the latest version by filing time is used. Listed when any of the four differs by $25,000 and 1% of the first year's expenses. Pairs explained by the order in which an amended return was filed are placed last.","limits":"Restating last year can follow from many things: an audit finished after the return was filed, an accounting standard changed, or two bodies merged. The careful way to show it is an amended return or a prior-period adjustment line, and the note column says when one is present. A changed opening balance with neither means the reader of either return alone cannot tell which figure is right. A short-form return carries fewer balances and is more often incomplete. A gap is a reason to ask for the audited statements, not a finding.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"year","t":"Year closed","type":"date"},{"k":"naClose","t":"Net assets at close, as filed","type":"usd"},{"k":"naOpen","t":"Net assets at next o